1. Creating and Renaming Files/Directories
Create a directory named test_dir using mkdir.
Inside test_dir, create an empty file called example.txt.
Rename example.txt to renamed_example.txt using mv
pornima.chakurkar@ LinuxCommandTutorial % mkdir test_dir
pornima.chakurkar@ LinuxCommandTutorial % cd test_dir 
pornima.chakurkar@ test_dir % touch example.txt.
pornima.chakurkar@ test_dir % ls
example.txt.
pornima.chakurkar@ test_dir % mv example.txt. example.txt 
pornima.chakurkar@ test_dir % ls
example.txt
pornima.chakurkar@ test_dir % mv example.txt renamed_example.txt
pornima.chakurkar@ test_dir % ls
renamed_example.txt
2. Viewing File Contents
Use cat to display the contents of /etc/passwd.
Display only the first 5 lines of /etc/passwd using head.
Display only the last 5 lines of /etc/passwd using tail.
Cat /etc/passwd
head -n 5  /etc/passwd
tale -n 5  /etc/passwd
3.Searching for Patterns
Use grep to find all lines containing the word "root" in /etc/passwd.

cat renamed_example.txt|grep map.put
            map.put(ch, map.getOrDefault(ch, 0) + 1);
